{#
  ============================================================================
  Recyclarr configuration - managed by Ansible role: media_acquisition
  ============================================================================

  Recyclarr pulls quality definitions, quality profiles and custom formats
  from the TRaSH Guides straight into Sonarr and Radarr, on the schedule set
  by CRON_SCHEDULE. That beats hand-maintaining scoring tables in two web
  UIs, and it means a rebuilt Pi lands on the same profiles as the old one.

  WHY THESE TEMPLATES, for a van:

  The Pi 5 has no hardware video encoder - it was physically removed, and only
  HEVC decode remains - so every Jellyfin transcode is software and competes
  with the gateway for CPU. The clients here are phones, tablets and laptop
  browsers, and browsers are the fussiest: they transcode HEVC and anything
  that is not stereo-friendly.

  So the goal is never to transcode at all, which means grabbing 1080p H.264.
  The TRaSH 1080p profiles below already score "x265 (HD)" heavily negative
  for exactly this reason, which is the behaviour we want rather than
  something to override.
#}
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
{% if media_acquisition.services.radarr.enabled %}
radarr:
  radarr-main:
    base_url: http://{{ media_acquisition.services.radarr.hostname }}:{{ media_acquisition.services.radarr.port }}
    api_key: {{ media_secrets.radarr }}

    delete_old_custom_formats: true
    replace_existing_custom_formats: true

    include:
      - template: radarr-quality-definition-movie
      - template: radarr-quality-profile-hd-bluray-web
      - template: radarr-custom-formats-hd-bluray-web

    quality_profiles:
      - name: {{ media_acquisition.profiles.radarr.quality_profile }}
        # Keep upgrading until the best allowed release is found, then stop.
        upgrade:
          allowed: true
        # 2160p is pointless here: nothing in the van can display it and it
        # would force a software transcode on every single play.
        min_format_score: 0
        quality_sort: top

{% endif %}
{% if media_acquisition.services.sonarr.enabled %}
sonarr:
  sonarr-main:
    base_url: http://{{ media_acquisition.services.sonarr.hostname }}:{{ media_acquisition.services.sonarr.port }}
    api_key: {{ media_secrets.sonarr }}

    delete_old_custom_formats: true
    replace_existing_custom_formats: true

    include:
      - template: sonarr-quality-definition-series
      - template: sonarr-v4-quality-profile-web-1080p
      - template: sonarr-v4-custom-formats-web-1080p

    quality_profiles:
      - name: {{ media_acquisition.profiles.sonarr.quality_profile }}
        upgrade:
          allowed: true
        min_format_score: 0
        quality_sort: top

{% endif %}
{#
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LANGUAGE SCORING IS NOT HERE, ON PURPOSE
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  English primary, German secondary, anything else only as a last resort.
  None of that is configured in this file, and it is not an oversight.

  Recyclarr cannot do anything at all until it has fetched the TRaSH Guides
  over the uplink. In a van that fetch is a coin toss - and when it loses, it
  does not skip the guide and apply the rest, it exits:

    [WRN] All references failed to fetch: Could not resolve host
    [ERR] Exiting due to fatal error

  That is what the 04:00 run had been doing every night, which is why both
  apps had zero custom formats and no language preference whatsoever while
  this file sat on disk looking correct.

  So the language policy is applied straight through the Sonarr and Radarr
  APIs by tasks/configure/languages.yml, which needs no internet. Quality and
  release scoring stay here, where an occasional successful run improves
  things and a failed run costs nothing.

  IF YOU ADD TRaSH LANGUAGE FORMATS ANYWAY, mind two things. Its formats are
  heavily NEGATIVE (Not German or English scores -35000), so pairing them
  with a profile minimum of 0 rejects the fallback release the policy is
  meant to allow. And `replace_existing_custom_formats: true` above means a
  profile template can reset formatItems, wiping the Ansible-applied scores -
  re-run `--tags media_config` afterwards to put them back. The real ids, if
  you want them:

    Radarr  86bc3115eb4e9873ac96904a4a68e19e  German
            f845be10da4f442654c13e1f2c3d6cd5  German DL
            4eadb75fb23d09dfc0a8e3f687e72287  Not German or English
    Sonarr  8a9fcdbb445f2add0505926df3bb7b8a  German
            ed51973a811f51985f14e2f6f290e47a  German DL
            133589380b89f8f8394320901529bac1  Not German or English
#}
